Yes, additions.ndjson can technically be forwarded to Splunk, but it would be only a partial historical feed—not a sufficient telemetry solution.
It can provide:
software_added
release_added
Vendor, artifact/version, commit time and initial security state
It cannot provide:
Later active/inactive/suppressed transitions—those are in scans.ndjson, not additions.ndjson
Libraries under vendor-catalog/library
Removals, sunset transitions, suppression renewal/expiration, or detailed failure reasons
Request initiation, approval/rejection, scan-start, workflow failure or time-to-approval events
Accurate current whitelist totals, because an originally added image may subsequently become inactive

Two important corrections:
metrics-catalog.yml does modify the catalog repository’s generated metrics/ files and .gitignore, although it does not modify vendor-catalog/.
Its push: main trigger applies to this automation repository, not automatically to pushes on RECORE.third-party-catalog. Catalog-only changes are generally picked up by the daily schedule.
Also, forwarding the complete regenerated additions.ndjson every day would create duplicate Splunk events unless the forwarder implements checkpoints or deterministic deduplication.

Recommendation: use additions.ndjson only for a one-time historical backfill or supplementary “artifact added” reporting. It should not be the primary feed for the full telemetry acceptance criteria. 
If this extraction approach is retained, it would at minimum need both additions and scans, library support, additional transition types, and incremental delivery.

Correct—Git history is not a good primary source for live Splunk telemetry.
Problems include:
Events arrive only after a catalog commit, so workflow failures and cancellations may never appear.
Request, approval, scan-start and failure-reason data may not exist in the catalog.
Commit time is not necessarily the actual event time.
Replaying full history is increasingly expensive and can resend duplicates.
Merge commits, generated metrics commits and history rewrites add noise or risk data loss.
Some catalog categories are currently omitted by the extractor.
Git history is still useful for:
A one-time historical backfill
Periodic reconciliation
Auditing catalog state changes
The stronger primary design is to emit events at the workflow’s authoritative write-time checkpoints, using the shared composite action, while keeping Git-history extraction only as a backup.
============
Yes—metrics-catalog.yml checks out the catalog with fetch-depth: 0, and the extractor walks every reachable commit that touched each supported catalog JSON file.
It therefore rebuilds DASHBOARD.html from the available Git history each time, rather than processing only changes since the previous run.
Important limitations:
Only vendor-catalog/containers/*.json, charts/*.json, and packages/*.json are included.
vendor-catalog/library/*.json is omitted.
Only history still reachable in Git can be reconstructed; rewritten or removed history is unavailable.
Renamed or deleted catalog files may not be handled as complete continuous history.
The dashboard reflects derived catalog changes, not workflow events that never produced a catalog commit.
======
It becomes progressively more expensive as the repository grows.
Every run:
Downloads the complete catalog Git history.
Finds every commit for every supported JSON file.
Executes git show for each historical version.
Parses and compares every reconstructed snapshot.
Regenerates all NDJSON files and the dashboard from scratch.
The fact that scans.ndjson and all-events.ndjson already exceed GitHub’s 100 MB limit indicates meaningful processing volume.
It is self-healing, but scales poorly. A better production design would process only commits since a stored checkpoint, with an occasional full-history reconciliation—not rebuild everything daily.
